Isopologues and isotopomers are two terms used to describe variations in molecules based on their isotopic composition. The key difference lies in whether the variations are in the number of atoms or the position of atoms:

1. **Isopologues**: Isopologues are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the number of atoms of a specific element. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_4 \) (ethane) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_2\text{D}_2 \) (deuterated ethane) are isotopologues because they have the same molecular formula but differ in the number of hydrogen atoms and deuterium atoms.

2. **Isottomers**: Isottomers are molecules that have the same molecular formula and the same arrangement of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_4 \) and \( \text{C}_2\text{H}_4 \) (where one hydrogen atom is replaced by deuterium) are isotomers because they have the same molecular formula and the same arrangement of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of one hydrogen atom.

In summary, the key difference is:
- Isopologues differ in the number of atoms of a specific element.
- Isotomers differ in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms.

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isopologues: Same molecular formula, different number of atoms of a specific element.} \\
&\text{Isotomers: Same molecular formula, same arrangement of atoms, different isotopic composition.}
\end{aligned}
}

